  "worked_solution": "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u,$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $\\frac{2}{3}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u & =\\frac{2}{3}(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v) & =\\frac{1}{2}(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2+0-3-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=\\frac{20}{-2}=-10.\n$$",
